why 1 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices rise into the F chord after the passing IV; the bass leaps up a fourth to the root.
why 1 3/4 LEADING_TONE The music turns to D minor (vi) instead of F. The alto's E falls to D, the root of the new chord, while F is in the soprano.
why 3 7/8 CROSSING The tenor passes C–B♭–A under the bass's held C into the cadence; then the bass drops to F.
why 5 0 OVERLAP Alto and tenor both move up, C–F and A–D, so the tenor passes where the alto was. They move together.
why 6 0 PROGRESSION The bass climbs G–A–B♭–C to the dominant; I⁶ is a passing chord on that line, not a step backward.
why 8 0 CROSSING The tenor drops an octave to E, under the bass's A, and climbs back E–F–G, making room for the alto's held D.
why 8 0 TENDENCY The alto holds D and falls to C♯ a beat later, as a suspension; it does resolve down by step.
why 8 1/8 CROSSING The tenor climbs to F under the bass's held A; on the next beat the bass drops an octave and is below again.
why 8 1/2 DOUBLING A deceptive turn: the A⁷ chord moves to B♭ instead of D minor. The alto's C♯ rises to D as a leading tone should, and the soprano falls E–D; both land on D.
